Kernel 1.1 — First Production Slice
Venue

the-cave

Capacity target
~100 total connected participants
includes:
producer
director
cast
crew
audience

That is a fine planning ceiling for the first slice.

Not because it must scale there immediately, but because it keeps you from designing something absurdly tiny.

Spatial structure
1. Fire

Primary focal element.

This is the first required shared stage object.

Purpose:

common point of attention
proves shared perception
anchors storytelling
2. Speaker position

Area near/in front of the fire where storyteller/actor performs.

3. Audience area

Standing-room-oriented public view zone.

Default:

sees fire
sees performer
does not see backstage tools
4. Backstage/tools area

Visible to cast/crew/director as needed.
Hidden from audience by default.

This is where:

controls live
future set-building tools live
notes/cues arrive
hidden elements originate
5. Producer box

Not required for first build, but structurally valid.
A privileged audience-facing view with optional oversight signals.

First required views
Audience view

Sees:

fire
performer
allowed reactions from others
minimal interface

Does not see:

backstage
director controls
hidden tools
Actor view

Sees:

fire
audience
some backstage/tool context
own cues if enabled
Director view

Sees:

everything relevant
audience reactions
backstage/tools
reveal controls
Producer view

Initially can just inherit director or privileged audience view.
No need to overbuild this first.

First exchange model

You answered the important thing:

there must be an exchange

So first build is not one-way broadcast.

It must support:

Outbound
performer tells story
fire is visible
Inbound
audience reacts

That makes it theater, not slideshow.

First audience reaction set

This is already enough:

clap
boo
cry
smile
laugh
startle
heart
thumbs_up
thumbs_down
cheer
standing_clap
note_card

That is a solid first reaction vocabulary.

Important split

These are not all the same type.

Immediate reactions

Fast, lightweight, ambient:

clap
boo
smile
laugh
startle
heart
thumbs_up
thumbs_down
cheer
standing_clap
Routed reactions

Need delivery logic:

note_card

That distinction matters.

Note cards

Good instinct.

A note card should not behave like open chat.

It should behave like:

react/note_card

With routing rules such as:

to crew
to director
to cast
maybe filtered by protocol

That preserves focus.

You are right that unrestricted audience comment can wreck attention.

So:

chat below can exist later
note cards are the first audience voice tool

That is cleaner and more theatrical anyway.

What the-cave proves

If the-cave works, then the system has proven:

shared focal stage
role-separated views
basic reveal/hide boundaries
performer-to-audience transmission
audience-to-production feedback
first real exchange

That is enough to justify the rest.

What not to build yet

Do not build yet:

cameras
black box modularity
marketplace
grades/contact logs
complicated producer admin suite
dynamic automated segments
full comment threads
media broadcasting stack beyond what the first cave needs

Those are all later layers.

My read on your hosting thought

You’re probably right that local/self-run instances matter early.

So for the-cave, think:

one producer-owned instance
modest participant count
one venue
one live session
constrained media expectations

That is sane.

